For your .NET 10 project, the best approach is the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package — it's battle-tested and provides a drop-in `IFileSystem` interface covering `File.*`, `Directory.*`, and `Path.*`.

## Setup

**1. Install packages:**
```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers  # test project only
```

**2. Register in DI** (in `Program.cs`):
```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

**3. Inject into your classes:**
```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions;

public class ReportGenerator(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string GenerateReport(string reportName)
    {
        var outputDir = "/tmp/reports";

        if (!fileSystem.Directory.Exists(outputDir))
            fileSystem.Directory.CreateDirectory(outputDir);

        var reportPath = fileSystem.Path.Combine(outputDir, $"{reportName}.txt");
        fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(reportPath, "content");

        return reportPath;
    }
}
```

**4. Test with `MockFileSystem`:**
```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/tmp/reports", new MockDirectoryData() }
});

var generator = new ReportGenerator(mockFs);
var path = generator.GenerateReport("daily");

Assert.True(mockFs.File.Exists(path));
```

This covers all three of your statics (`File.WriteAllText`, `File.ReadAllText`, `Directory.Exists`) through a single `IFileSystem` injection. No custom wrappers needed.

> **Note:** Your `ReportGenerator` also uses `DateTime.UtcNow` and `Environment.*` — for those, inject `TimeProvider` (built-in on .NET 10) and a custom `IEnvironmentProvider` respectively. Let me know if you'd like guidance on those too.
